WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.808 --> 00:00:09.074
Napoleon believed that destiny had chosen him for greatness.

2
00:00:10.115 --> 00:00:15.218
A dictator, military genius, and social reformer, he inspired both awe and hatred.

3
00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:21.543
His ambition brought him amazing military and political success, yet his tactics were often ruthless.

4
00:00:22.484 --> 00:00:29.124
Nearly two hundred years after his death, scholars still debate Napoleon's motives and the value of his methods.

5
00:00:29.164 --> 00:00:35.569
He remains a controversial figure because despite his historic achievements, he ended his days in exile, branded an

6
00:00:35.589 --> 00:00:36.830
"enemy of humanity."

7
00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:43.915
Napoleon Bonaparte was born on August 15, 1769, on the Mediterranean island of Corsica.

8
00:00:44.795 --> 00:00:50.259
The rugged island fought unsuccessfully for independence first from Italy and then from France.

9
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.684
Napoleon's parents supported the Corsican struggle, teaching their eight children the value of independence.

10
00:00:57.696 --> 00:01:00.817
Although descended from nobility, the family was not rich.

11
00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:06.920
They used political connections to get Napoleon accepted into a French military academy at the age of nine.

12
00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:13.783
There, his thick Corsican accent and broken French made him an outcast among his classmates, many of whom

13
00:01:13.843 --> 00:01:14.943
were wealthy nobility.

14
00:01:15.804 --> 00:01:21.686
Napoleon was a dedicated student, however, and because of his excellent academic record, he graduated at the age

15
00:01:21.726 --> 00:01:25.488
of 16, joining the Royal Artillery as a junior officer.

16
00:01:26.364 --> 00:01:30.565
Napoleon began his military career as an artillery commander in the south of France.

17
00:01:31.526 --> 00:01:37.287
He spent his wages on books, instead of wine and women, and impressed senior officers, who noticed his

18
00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:38.728
intelligence and discipline.

19
00:01:39.768 --> 00:01:46.690
In 1789, the French Revolution began—commoners were rising up against the monarchy that had ruled France for centuries.

20
00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.652
Napoleon embraced the ideals of the revolution, which aimed to end economic and social divides between the nobility

21
00:01:53.732 --> 00:01:54.392
and the commoners.

22
00:01:55.212 --> 00:01:57.594
But he also saw a chance to advance his career.

23
00:01:58.454 --> 00:02:05.179
In 1793, the 24-year-old Napoleon was promoted to major and sent to Toulon, a French town that British

24
00:02:05.239 --> 00:02:07.680
troops had invaded in hopes of ending the revolution.

25
00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:13.744
When Napoleon drove the British out, the victory ignited his ambition and fueled his sense of destiny.

26
00:02:14.505 --> 00:02:18.728
Word of Napoleon's success reached Paris, and he rose quickly in the military.

27
00:02:19.528 --> 00:02:25.696
When rebels who wanted to restore the monarchy planned to take over the republic in October 1795, Napoleon

28
00:02:25.736 --> 00:02:29.859
was put in charge of defending the Tuileries Palace, the offices of the government.

29
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.963
His forces placed cannons in the streets around the palace and met the rebels with brutal cannon fire.

30
00:02:37.104 --> 00:02:41.207
After only 15 minutes, hundreds of rebels were dead and the others had fled.

31
00:02:42.088 --> 00:02:48.692
Napoleon, just 26 years old, became a national hero, celebrated for crushing the uprising with force and speed.

32
00:02:49.473 --> 00:02:54.604
He was soon made commander of the French army and led his troops against Italy and Austria, where

33
00:02:54.624 --> 00:02:59.226
his use of artillery and deception created tactical advantages that crippled the enemy.

34
00:02:59.966 --> 00:03:05.929
He was now a living legend—a general who slept little, worked constantly, and captured the love of his

35
00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:08.230
soldiers by leading the way into battle himself.

36
00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:14.553
In 1799, Napoleon returned from a military campaign in Egypt to a France in turmoil.

37
00:03:15.293 --> 00:03:18.895
The republic was running out of money and being threatened by foreign powers.

38
00:03:19.815 --> 00:03:25.245
The population was angry with the new government, and some politicians believed that the military must be put

39
00:03:25.285 --> 00:03:27.746
in power to prevent the return of the monarchy.

40
00:03:28.747 --> 00:03:31.288
Napoleon was asked to help overthrow the government.

41
00:03:31.868 --> 00:03:36.010
Sensing an opportunity to increase his power, he agreed to participate in a coup.

42
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:42.732
A new government was created, and Napoleon seized control, becoming head of the republic, or "first consul."

43
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:48.115
Acting as dictator of France, he conducted himself like a soldier even in politics.

44
00:03:48.875 --> 00:03:55.619
He ruled the country through censorship and surveillance, shutting down many Paris newspapers and using secret police to

45
00:03:55.659 --> 00:03:57.980
spy on citizens from all walks of life.

46
00:03:58.681 --> 00:04:01.202
People who opposed him faced prison or death.

47
00:04:01.923 --> 00:04:06.045
Napoleon, now thirty years old, held France in an iron grip.

48
00:04:06.966 --> 00:04:13.350
Despite Napoleon's harsh tactics, his glory as a military genius and hero of the revolution had not been

49
00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.232
forgotten, and many people in France supported him.

50
00:04:17.013 --> 00:04:21.696
Their faith seemed to be rewarded when he began a program of social and governmental reform.

51
00:04:22.708 --> 00:04:30.394
Napoleon made sweeping changes to the French banking, judicial, and educational systems, ensuring equality for all French citizens.

52
00:04:31.255 --> 00:04:35.919
The civil laws that were created at his direction are known as the Napoleonic Code and remain in

53
00:04:36.019 --> 00:04:36.579
use today.

54
00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:42.804
As commander of what was then the largest army in Europe, Napoleon also gave soldiers the right to

55
00:04:42.904 --> 00:04:45.667
rise in rank by merit rather than social class.

56
00:04:46.547 --> 00:04:53.424
Acclaimed by a grateful nation, Napoleon drew upon his sense of destiny and named himself emperor in 1804.

57
00:04:54.265 --> 00:04:57.546
As emperor, Napoleon aimed to expand France's boundaries.

58
00:04:58.207 --> 00:05:03.609
Ambitious to control all of Europe, he achieved his greatest military victory in the Battle of Austerlitz.

59
00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:10.713
Fighting against Austrian and Russian troops, he displayed tactical brilliance by tricking the enemy into thinking he had

60
00:05:10.833 --> 00:05:12.093
fewer troops than he did.

61
00:05:12.994 --> 00:05:17.095
The French lost 9,000 men, while Russia and Austria together lost 25,000.

62
00:05:19.092 --> 00:05:24.777
Napoleon insisted that destiny would always lead him to victory, and over the next years, he fought in

63
00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:28.540
Prussia, Poland, and Russia, claiming new territories for France.

64
00:05:29.421 --> 00:05:34.205
But by 1812, the tide was turning against him and his popularity was falling.

65
00:05:34.965 --> 00:05:40.710
Wars in Spain and Portugal were going badly, and in the fall and winter of 1812, Napoleon lost

66
00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:46.535
more than half a million of his soldiers while retreating from an ill-fated campaign in Russia; they starved,

67
00:05:46.855 --> 00:05:49.948
froze, deserted, or were captured by enemy forces.

68
00:05:50.869 --> 00:05:57.471
By 1814, Austria, Prussia, Russia, and Great Britain had allied to attack France and overthrow Napoleon.

69
00:05:58.311 --> 00:06:03.072
When Paris was captured in April 1814, Napoleon abdicated the throne.

70
00:06:04.213 --> 00:06:07.493
Only Napoleon's fame and power kept him from being executed.

71
00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:11.795
He was banished to the Italian island of Elba and guarded day and night.

72
00:06:12.735 --> 00:06:18.369
Letters and newspapers enabled him to follow events in France, however, and when word came that the monarchy

73
00:06:18.409 --> 00:06:20.771
would be restored, he escaped from the island.

74
00:06:21.592 --> 00:06:27.196
He returned to France in 1815, arriving in Paris on March 20 in the company of a loyal

75
00:06:27.316 --> 00:06:33.460
army. Napoleon was welcomed back by the French people and reclaimed the title of emperor, but the countries

76
00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.282
allied against him began to gather troops for an attack.

77
00:06:37.143 --> 00:06:42.587
He faced them in June 1815 in the Battle of Waterloo, which resulted in a crushing defeat for

78
00:06:42.627 --> 00:06:43.708
Napoleon and his army.

79
00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:49.068
One hundred days after his triumphant return to France, he was forced to abdicate again.

80
00:06:49.929 --> 00:06:51.971
He was banished from France and sent to St.

81
00:06:51.991 --> 00:06:55.394
Helena, a remote and inescapable island in the Atlantic Ocean.

82
00:06:56.154 --> 00:07:00.358
After six years in exile, Napoleon died on May 5, 1821.

83
00:07:01.459 --> 00:07:06.724
More books have been written about Napoleon Bonaparte than about any other figure in recent history.

84
00:07:07.624 --> 00:07:11.428
Scholars are still trying to understand whether he was a tyrant or a visionary.

85
00:07:12.296 --> 00:07:17.438
Millions of people died on his battlefields, and millions of people benefited from his reforms.

86
00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:24.902
Many European nations still use the Napoleonic Code, and his military tactics are studied by armed forces around

87
00:07:24.942 --> 00:07:25.362
the world.

88
00:07:26.182 --> 00:07:32.005
Napoleon's belief in destiny drove him to greatness, but his hunger for power may have destroyed his chance

89
00:07:32.065 --> 00:07:33.706
to achieve lasting glory.
